From a989aa05654d64eb2cbfc4e05cc28fc01179c9bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gnanaprakash Elumalai <gelumala@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 07:55:38 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] bwl, controller: fix unassoc RSSI cancellation, scope query,
 fix response radio resolution
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A duplicate unassoc-metrics query arriving mid-scan (~5s re-query vs. ~600ms
ROC scan) destroyed the in-flight measurement: first_channel stayed 0, the
emptiness guard fired on the wrong list, and trigger_roc_scan(0) failed and
wiped every pending measurement, not just the duplicate's. Track each call's
own MACs, key the early-return/cleanup off that list, and serialise
m_pending_unassoc_measurements (shared by the ap_manager and monitor threads).

Separately, unassoc_sta_rcpi_query re-queried the entire persistent
unassociated-STA DB on every registration, turning N requests into N*M CMDUs
across all (agent, operating_class) pairs. Add
get_unassociated_stations_stats_scoped() to restrict each RPC to only the
pairs it just registered; other callers keep the unscoped behaviour.

Finally, handle_cmdu_1905_unassociated_station_link_metric_response() matched
a response's operating_class/channel against each radio's advertised
scan_capabilities.operating_classes to find which radio answered. When that
map is stale or missing the reported class/channel entirely, the lookup
failed and the response was dropped (ERROR "has no radio that supports
operating_class X or channel Y") even though the query was sent to, and
answered by, a real, currently-tuned radio. Add a further fallback matching
the radio's live operating channel via database.get_radio_wifi_channel() —
the same basis unassoc_sta_rcpi_query() already uses to pick which radio to
query in the first place.

Signed-off-by: Gnanaprakash Elumalai <gelumala@qti.qualcomm.com>
